# FastEddy v5.0.1 (kegonsa fork) — PORTABLE SEED IMAGE, CUDA 13.0, Blackwell-capable
# =============================================================================
# Kegonsa Solar Array flux-footprint project. Built to be pulled onto a rented
# multi-GPU box and run with ONE command, with no clone, no build and no chance
# of the wrong commit:
#
#     docker run --gpus all -v /out:/out <image> run_seeds --gpu-count 16
#
# THIS IS NOT THE `Dockerfile` NEXT TO IT. That one is CUDA 11.8 and TOOLCHAIN
# ONLY -- it contains no project files, compiles nothing, and every result this
# project has published came out of it. It is kept unchanged as the record of
# how those results were produced. This file is the deployable one: newer
# toolkit, code baked in, FastEddy compiled at image build time.
#
# WHY THE TOOLKIT MOVED -- THE BLOCKING FACT
# ------------------------------------------
# nvcc 11.8's highest target is sm_90. The RTX 5090 is sm_120, an architecture
# that did not exist when 11.8 was written, and CUDA's forward compatibility
# runs through PTX JIT rather than through the compiler: a toolkit cannot emit
# code -- SASS or PTX -- for a virtual architecture it has never heard of.
# 11.8's newest PTX is compute_90, so an 11.8 binary cannot even JIT onto
# sm_120. It fails at cuModuleLoad, before any physics.
#
# PROJECT_BRIEF.md's "CUDA 11.8 (do not upgrade)" was written when sm_89 was the only
# target that mattered and 11.8 was the FIRST release that had it -- a floor,
# not a ceiling. That pin is superseded HERE and only here. What the upgrade
# changed, and what it measurably did not, is FASTEDDY_TRAPS.md 23.
#
# WHY 13.0 RATHER THAN 12.8. 12.8 is the first toolkit with sm_120 and would
# have done; 13.0 was tried first because it matches the driver generation on
# the target boxes (r580), so nothing anywhere in the stack is running in a
# compatibility mode. It built, so it shipped. The two frictions 13.0 has that
# 12.8 does not are both recorded below and both are one-line fixes.
#
# ARCHITECTURES: SEVEN, ALL REAL SASS, NO PTX -- AND THE "NO PTX" IS FORCED
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The intent was real SASS for sm_89 and sm_120 plus compute_90/compute_80 PTX
# as a JIT fallback. MEASURED, and it does not work, silently:
#
#     nvcc -gencode ...code=compute_90 -dc a.cu -o a.o   ->  a.o HAS the PTX
#     nvcc -gencode ...code=compute_90 a.o -dlink -o dl.o -> dl.o HAS NONE
#
# FastEddy is built with SEPARATE COMPILATION (-dc then -dlink) because it has
# __device__ functions crossing translation units, and `nvcc -dlink` drops
# every PTX image from the fatbin WITHOUT A WARNING. The executable's fatbin
# comes from the device link, so a PTX fallback cannot exist for this binary.
# The same flags on a whole-program compile keep the PTX, which is what makes
# the behaviour easy to assume and hard to notice.
#
# So the fallback is REAL CODE for every architecture instead, which is the
# better answer anyway for exactly the reason a PTX fallback was wanted to be
# avoided: hydro-core kernels are register-heavy, and JIT defers register
# allocation to the driver at load time where it is invisible and unmeasurable.
#
#   sm_75  Turing    T4, RTX 2080          -- the oldest CUDA 13.0 supports
#   sm_80  Ampere    A100
#   sm_86  Ampere    A10, A40, RTX 3090
#   sm_89  Ada       RTX 4080/4090, L40S   -- the local smoke-test card
#   sm_90  Hopper    H100, H200
#   sm_100 Blackwell B200, GB200
#   sm_120 Blackwell RTX 5090, RTX PRO     -- THE DEPLOYMENT TARGET
#
# Turing through Blackwell, natively, with no JIT anywhere. Cost: 83 s of build
# and an 18 MB binary against 5 MB for one architecture. Anything older than
# sm_75 will not run, and docker/verify_image.sh says so at startup rather than
# at the first kernel launch.
#
# BITWISE REPRODUCIBILITY DOES NOT SURVIVE THIS, AND IS NOT MEANT TO.
# FastEddy is not bitwise reproducible run-to-run on ONE GPU (~1e-4 relative in
# velocity after 200 steps -- PROJECT_BRIEF.md), so it certainly is not across a
# toolkit change or an architecture change. The claim this image makes is the
# one that matters and the one that was measured, against that same floor:
#
#     bin/test_toolkit_parity.py, 200 steps, jobs30/seed_nbl-deep_a015, one GPU
#     |11.8 - 13.0| against |11.8 - 11.8| :  u 0.97x  v 1.03x  w 1.05x  th 1.12x
#
# The toolkit change is worth no more than re-running the same case. Seeds are
# turbulence REALISATIONS; do not diff two of them.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 1: toolchain. Its own stage so an edit below this line does not re-run
# 4 GB of apt and pip.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PINNED BY DIGEST, NOT BY TAG. `13.0.1-devel-ubuntu22.04` is mutable and NVIDIA re-pushes
# it; the digest is what makes "rebuild this commit and get this toolchain" true rather
# than true-on-the-day. Update the digest and the tag together, deliberately.
FROM nvidia/cuda:13.0.1-devel-ubuntu22.04@sha256:93a8d207db5aaa6384f834a6bf70d417433f709e61b57a91e7cc99c16172f49c AS toolchain

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 1: compilers, MPI, NetCDF/HDF5.
#
# UBUNTU 22.04, DELIBERATELY, AND NOT 24.04. Every apt package below is a CPU
# library CUDA does not touch: OpenMPI 4.1.2, NetCDF-C 4.8.1, HDF5 1.10.7, and
# gcc 11.4. Holding the distro fixed holds all four fixed, so the ONLY thing
# this image changes against the 11.8 one is the CUDA toolkit. If a number
# moves there is exactly one candidate. Moving to 24.04 would have changed the
# MPI, the NetCDF and the compiler at the same time and made the parity
# measurement above uninterpretable.
#
# AND THAT CLAIM IS ONLY AS STRONG AS WHAT IS PINNED. STATED PLAINLY:
# the BASE is pinned by digest (above), so the toolkit, the distro, gcc, MPI and
# NetCDF-C are fixed by construction -- those are the ones the FastEddy binary
# links against, so the parity measurement is reproducible. The pip layers are
# NOT pinned: `numpy`, `scipy`, `netCDF4` and the rest float. Today they match
# the 11.8 image exactly (numpy 2.2.6, scipy 1.15.3, netCDF4 1.7.4 in both,
# checked), but that is a coincidence of build date, not a construction. It is
# accepted because those packages are the ANALYSIS stack -- they read a dump and
# score it; none of them is in the LES, and a seed's physics does not pass
# through them. IMAGE_PROVENANCE.txt records the versions that were actually
# installed, so a number that moves later can at least be attributed.
#
# AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THAT MEASUREMENT IN A WAY THAT IS EASY TO MISS.
# hydro_core.c:1881 draws the initial theta perturbation with rand(), seeded at
# FastEddy.c:113 by `srand(mpi_rank_world + 12345)` -- a FIXED seed at one rank.
# So the perturbation field is reproducible, but only for a given libc: the
# sequence rand() returns for seed 12345 is a property of glibc, not of the C
# standard. Same distro, same glibc, same initial condition on both sides of
# the comparison. A distro bump would have changed the initial condition and
# the parity test would have been measuring rand().
#
# (An earlier version of this comment said "nothing in FastEddy ever calls
# srand(), so the cold start uses glibc's default seed-1 sequence". That is
# false -- FastEddy.c:113 -- and it is recorded rather than quietly replaced
# because the CONCLUSION survived the wrong mechanism, which is exactly how a
# wrong mechanism gets to stay in a file.)
#
# The 11.8-era gcc ceiling is gone (nvcc 13.0 accepts gcc 8-15, and 22.04 ships
# 11.4), so gcc-11 is now a choice rather than a constraint. It is kept because
# it is what the published results were compiled with.
#
# NetCDF/HDF5/MPI needed NO rebuild for the toolkit change, which was the one
# piece of anticipated friction that did not materialise: none of them link
# against CUDA.
#
# Stock OpenMPI, not a CUDA-aware build. FastEddy hands raw device pointers to
# MPI_Isend/MPI_Irecv under hydroBCs==2 with no numProcs==1 bypass, which looks
# like it needs CUDA-awareness -- so it was tested rather than assumed (see the
# 11.8 Dockerfile): at one rank the two builds differ only at the model's own
# nondeterminism floor. Every seed is numProcsX = numProcsY = 1.
#
# openssh-server AND rsync ARE FOR VAST.AI, AND THEY ARE NOT DECORATION.
# Vast's SSH launch mode "injects setup scripts into your existing Docker image,
# which means your image's original entrypoint is replaced", and requires the
# image to be "compatible with typical ssh daemon setup" -- without saying
# whether it supplies sshd or expects one. Installing it costs ~1 MB and removes
# a whole class of instance-will-not-come-up failure that is only discoverable
# after renting. rsync is how ~2.2 GB of seeds come back over that same SSH.
#
# Fortran MPI bindings are REQUIRED even though FastEddy has no Fortran source:
# its C code broadcasts with MPI_INTEGER and MPI_CHARACTER (60 occurrences),
# which are Fortran datatype handles. --disable-mpi-fortran removes them and
# the model aborts in MPI_Bcast with MPI_ERR_TYPE. Verified by doing it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        build-essential \
        gfortran \
        make \
        wget \
        ca-certificates \
        file \
        git \
        bc \
        procps \
        openssh-server \
        rsync \
        openmpi-bin \
        libopenmpi-dev \
        libhdf5-openmpi-dev \
        libnetcdf-dev \
        libnetcdff-dev \
        libpnetcdf-dev \
        pnetcdf-bin \
        netcdf-bin \
        python3 \
        python3-pip \
        python3-dev \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Fail the build here rather than at model startup.
RUN ompi_info | grep -q "Fort mpif.h: yes" \
    || { echo "FATAL: MPI lacks Fortran bindings; FastEddy's MPI_INTEGER/MPI_CHARACTER broadcasts would fail."; exit 1; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 2: the analysis stack. The host has no scipy, so every gate, every
# footprint and every acceptance check in this project runs inside this image.
# Mirrors the 11.8 image exactly, plus scikit-image (SimGrid.py imports
# skimage.measure.block_reduce and NCAR's own environment.yml omits it).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        numpy \
        scipy \
        matplotlib \
        netCDF4 \
        h5py \
        xarray \
        pandas \
        dask \
        scikit-image \
        mpi4py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Layer 3: HRRR pseudo-sounding retrieval (bin/hrrr_sounding.py).
#
# A SEED NEEDS NONE OF THIS -- a seed is a flat uniform spin-up with an empty
# topoFile and an empty inFile, so it reads no sounding, no terrain and no land
# cover. It is here because the same image also runs corpus CASES, which do.
#
# `eccodes` is the pip wheel, which ships its own libeccodes; the apt package
# is deliberately NOT installed, because the two disagree on definitions paths
# and cfgrib picks whichever it finds first. One source of eccodes only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN pip3 install --no-cache-dir \
        "eccodes>=1.7" \
        "cfgrib>=0.9.10" \
        "herbie-data>=2024.3.0" \
        "pyproj>=3.6" \
        s3fs \
        scikit-learn \
    && python3 -c "import cfgrib, herbie; print('cfgrib', cfgrib.__version__, 'herbie', herbie.__version__)"

# OMPI_MCA_plm=isolated: OpenMPI's default launcher is `rsh`, which aborts
# hunting for an ssh binary a single-node container has no use for.
# OMPI_ALLOW_RUN_AS_ROOT: the image declares no USER, so a container started
# without --user runs as root, and OpenMPI refuses to launch as root without
# these two. The host path uses --user and never needed them.
ENV CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda \
    MPI_ROOT=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/openmpi \
    NETCDF_ROOT=/usr \
    OMPI_MCA_plm=isolated \
    OMPI_ALLOW_RUN_AS_ROOT=1 \
    OMPI_ALLOW_RUN_AS_ROOT_CONFIRM=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage 2: the image. Code baked in, FastEddy compiled, SASS asserted.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM toolchain AS flux

# THE ARCHITECTURE LIST IS ONE STRING, USED TWICE. The Makefile threads
# ARCH_CU_FLAGS through BOTH the -dc compile rule (Makefile:161) and the -dlink
# step (Makefile:196), and a device link whose gencode list disagrees with the
# objects' produces a binary that links and then finds no image for the running
# device. Passing the whole list through the one variable makes the two agree
# by construction -- which is also why no Makefile edit is needed.
ARG FE_GENCODE="-gencode arch=compute_75,code=sm_75 -gencode arch=compute_80,code=sm_80 -gencode arch=compute_86,code=sm_86 -gencode arch=compute_89,code=sm_89 -gencode arch=compute_90,code=sm_90 -gencode arch=compute_100,code=sm_100 -gencode arch=compute_120,code=sm_120"
ARG FE_STD="-std=c++17"
# PTX FOR liblpdm ONLY, AND IT IS EVIDENCE RATHER THAN A FEATURE.
# liblpdm.so is ONE translation unit, so it is compiled WHOLE-PROGRAM and its PTX
# survives -- while FastEddy's identical request does not survive `nvcc -dlink`.
# Asking for it here makes that contrast checkable on the shipped image itself
# (`cuobjdump --list-ptx` on the two files), instead of only in a throwaway test.
# It also gives the analysis library a genuine JIT fallback, which costs nothing.
ARG LPDM_PTX="-gencode arch=compute_80,code=compute_80 -gencode arch=compute_120,code=compute_120"
ARG GIT_COMMIT=unknown
ARG GIT_COMMIT_FE=unknown
ARG BUILD_DATE=unknown

WORKDIR /flux
COPY . /flux/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FastEddy, compiled into the image.
#
# The fork's Makefile is written for NCAR's Casper/Derecho module environment,
# where the `ncarcompilers` wrapper injects every include and library path. Off
# it, three lines are wrong -- and all three are plain `=` assignments, so make
# command-line overrides win and THE FORK'S MAKEFILE IS NOT EDITED:
#
#   Makefile:39  ARCH_CU_FLAGS  = -arch=sm_80                  # A100 only
#   Makefile:46  OTHER_INCLUDES = -I${NCAR_ROOT_MPI}/include    # -> "-I/include"
#   Makefile:48  TEST_LDFLAGS   = -L.                           # no lib paths
#
# A FOURTH OVERRIDE IS NEW AND IS THE FIRST OF CUDA 13.0's TWO FRICTIONS.
# Makefile:40 sets `-std=c++11` inside TEST_CU_CFLAGS. CUDA 13.0 ships CCCL
# (CUB/Thrust/libcu++), and CCCL now HARD-REQUIRES C++17:
#
#   cccl/cub/util_cpp_dialect.cuh:88: error: CUB requires at least C++17.
#   cccl/cuda/std/__internal/cpp_dialect.h:40: libcu++ requires at least C++17.
#   cccl/thrust/detail/config/cpp_dialect.h:78: Thrust requires at least C++17.
#
# FastEddy reaches CCCL through exactly one include -- fecuda_PlugIns.cu:17
# pulls <cub/cub.cuh> for cub::BlockReduce, which implements
# cuda_singleRankHorizSlabMeans, i.e. the slab means the subsidence forcing
# uses. It is on the physics path, so it cannot simply be dropped.
#
# The dialect is RAISED rather than the diagnostic suppressed. CCCL offers
# -DCCCL_IGNORE_DEPRECATED_CPP_DIALECT, and that would be exactly the
# "suppressed warning" this build refuses to have: CCCL says "requires", not
# "prefers". C++17 changes no floating-point semantics, and the parity
# measurement quoted at the top of this file is what establishes that in fact
# rather than in principle. TEST_CU_CFLAGS is a plain `=` too, and the
# override is written with $(...) so make still expands DEFINES/INCLUDES.
#
# Serial. The FastEddy_devlink.o rule has no prerequisites and begins with
# `rm -rf`, which races under -j.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eux; \
    cd /flux/FastEddy-model-5.0.1/SRC/FEMAIN; \
    MK="TEST_CC=mpicc \
        ARCH_CU_FLAGS=${FE_GENCODE} \
        OTHER_INCLUDES=-I${MPI_ROOT}/include -I${CUDA_HOME}/include -I${NETCDF_ROOT}/include"; \
    make clean TEST_CC=mpicc "ARCH_CU_FLAGS=${FE_GENCODE}" \
        "OTHER_INCLUDES=-I${MPI_ROOT}/include -I${CUDA_HOME}/include -I${NETCDF_ROOT}/include" \
        "TEST_LDFLAGS=-L." || true; \
    make \
        TEST_CC=mpicc \
        "ARCH_CU_FLAGS=${FE_GENCODE}" \
        "TEST_CU_CFLAGS=\$(ARCH_CU_FLAGS) -m64 ${FE_STD} \$(DEFINES) \$(INCLUDES) \$(OTHER_INCLUDES)" \
        "OTHER_INCLUDES=-I${MPI_ROOT}/include -I${CUDA_HOME}/include -I${NETCDF_ROOT}/include" \
        "TEST_LDFLAGS=-L. -L${CUDA_HOME}/lib64 -L${MPI_ROOT}/lib -L${NETCDF_ROOT}/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu" \
        2>&1 | tee /flux/build_fasteddy.log; \
    test -x /flux/FastEddy-model-5.0.1/SRC/FEMAIN/FastEddy

# NO WARNING IS SUPPRESSED AND NO NEW WARNING IS INTRODUCED -- and that is
# asserted rather than asserted-in-a-comment. The build carries upstream's
# -Wall on all host C and emits exactly NINE warnings, every one of them a
# pre-existing upstream printf format mismatch (%u for size_t, %d for size_t,
# a zero-length format string). The CUDA 11.8 build of the same tree emits the
# SAME NINE, from the same files with the same messages. If a tenth appears,
# the build stops here rather than letting it scroll past in a log.
# THE PATTERN HAS TO MATCH EVERY SHAPE A WARNING CAN TAKE HERE, and its first version
# matched one. `^\.\./.*: warning:` anchors on `../`, so it saw NONE of:
#   FastEddy.c:120:5: warning: ...        (FEMAIN is compiled from inside itself, no ../)
#   cuda_sgsTurbDevice.cu(88): warning #177-D: ...   (nvcc/cicc device-side form)
#   nvcc warning : ...  /  ptxas warning : ...
# A gate that cannot see two of the three formats is not a gate. And a COUNT cannot see a
# substitution: nine warnings that are a DIFFERENT nine would pass. Compare the SET.
#
# `.*` AND NOT `[^\n]*`. POSIX ERE has no \n escape inside a bracket expression, so GNU
# grep reads that class as "not a backslash and not the letter n" and TRUNCATES every
# diagnostic at its first n -- "warning: format '%u' expects argume". The baseline file
# would have been generated already truncated and the comparison would have compared two
# truncations. (The development host's grep is ugrep, which DOES accept it there, so the
# same command behaved differently inside and outside the image -- which is how this was
# found.) grep -o is per-line anyway, so `.*` is both correct and simpler.
RUN set -eux; \
    grep -oiE '(^|[[:space:]])[^[:space:]]+[:(][0-9]+[):][^[:space:]]*[[:space:]]*warning.*' \
        /flux/build_fasteddy.log \
      | sed -E 's/[0-9]+/N/g; s/^[[:space:]]+//' | sort -u > /flux/warnings_seen.txt; \
    echo "distinct diagnostics:"; sed 's/^/  /' /flux/warnings_seen.txt; \
    grep -v '^#' /flux/docker/expected_warnings.txt | grep -v '^$' > /flux/warnings_expected.txt; \
    diff -u /flux/warnings_expected.txt /flux/warnings_seen.txt \
      || { echo "FATAL: the warning set differs from the CUDA 11.8 baseline in"; \
           echo "       docker/expected_warnings.txt. NOTHING here is suppressed -- if the"; \
           echo "       new line is a real pre-existing upstream warning, add it there and"; \
           echo "       say so; if it is new, it is the toolkit and must be understood."; exit 1; }; \
    ! grep -qiE '^(nvcc|ptxas) warning' /flux/build_fasteddy.log \
      || { echo "FATAL: nvcc/ptxas emitted a warning"; exit 1; }

# The GPU-resident LPDM as a shared library. One translation unit, two
# front-ends: the same .cu is linked into FastEddy for production and built
# here for the acceptance suite, so both paths exercise the same integrator.
# Not on a seed's path; built because this image is also the analysis image.
# WHOLE-PROGRAM here (no -dc), so unlike FastEddy this one CAN carry PTX -- and
# ${LPDM_PTX} asks for it, which is what makes the -dlink finding reproducible
# FROM THE IMAGE: cuobjdump --list-ptx on liblpdm.so lists compute_80 and
# compute_120, and on FastEddy lists nothing, from the same toolkit and the same
# kind of request. The difference is separate vs whole-program compilation and
# nothing else.
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /flux/lib; \
    nvcc -O3 ${FE_STD} ${FE_GENCODE} ${LPDM_PTX} -Xcompiler -fPIC -shared \
         -I /flux/FastEddy-model-5.0.1/SRC/LPDM/CUDA \
         /flux/FastEddy-model-5.0.1/SRC/LPDM/CUDA/cuda_lpdmDevice.cu \
         -o /flux/lib/liblpdm.so 2>&1 | tee /flux/build_lpdm.log; \
    test -s /flux/lib/liblpdm.so

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASSERT ON THE ARTIFACT, NOT ON THE FLAGS. PROJECT_BRIEF.md's most expensive standing
# rule, and this project has paid for it four separate times. `-gencode ...
# code=sm_120` on the command line is a REQUEST; what the binary carries is the
# answer. cuobjdump reads the fatbin out of the ELF, so the build fails HERE if
# an architecture is missing rather than on a rented box at the first kernel.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eux; \
    BIN=/flux/FastEddy-model-5.0.1/SRC/FEMAIN/FastEddy; \
    cuobjdump --list-elf "$BIN" | sed 's/.*\.\(sm_[0-9]*\)\.cubin/\1/' | sort -u > /flux/sass_elf.txt; \
    (cuobjdump --list-ptx "$BIN" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/.*\.sm_\([0-9]*\)\.ptx/compute_\1/' | sort -u || true) > /flux/sass_ptx.txt; \
    echo "--- SASS in FastEddy ---"; cat /flux/sass_elf.txt; \
    echo "--- PTX in FastEddy (expected EMPTY: nvcc -dlink drops it) ---"; cat /flux/sass_ptx.txt; \
    # NOTE THE EXTRACTION: cuobjdump names a PTX image `<file>.<n>.sm_XX.ptx`, with the
    # `sm_` prefix, NOT `compute_XX.ptx`. Matching on `compute_` returns nothing for a
    # binary that HAS PTX -- which is how the liblpdm assertion below first fired against
    # a library that carried exactly the two images it was asking for. `--list-elf` and
    # `--list-ptx` name things differently and neither is guessable. \
    for arch in sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_89 sm_90 sm_100 sm_120; do \
      grep -qx "$arch" /flux/sass_elf.txt || { echo "FATAL: no $arch SASS in the binary"; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    cuobjdump --list-elf /flux/lib/liblpdm.so | sed 's/.*\.\(sm_[0-9]*\)\.cubin/\1/' | sort -u > /flux/sass_elf_lpdm.txt; \
    (cuobjdump --list-ptx /flux/lib/liblpdm.so 2>/dev/null | sed 's/.*\.sm_\([0-9]*\)\.ptx/compute_\1/' | sort -u || true) > /flux/sass_ptx_lpdm.txt; \
    grep -qx sm_120 /flux/sass_elf_lpdm.txt || { echo "FATAL: no sm_120 SASS in liblpdm.so"; exit 1; }; \
    grep -qx sm_89  /flux/sass_elf_lpdm.txt || { echo "FATAL: no sm_89 SASS in liblpdm.so"; exit 1; }; \
    # THE CONTRAST IS ASSERTED, because it is the evidence for the -dlink finding and a
    # claim in three files. Same toolkit, same kind of -gencode request, opposite outcome:
    # whole-program keeps PTX, separately-compiled-then-device-linked does not.
    grep -qx compute_120 /flux/sass_ptx_lpdm.txt \
      || { echo "FATAL: liblpdm.so carries no compute_120 PTX, so the whole-program-vs--dlink"; \
           echo "       contrast documented in FASTEDDY_TRAPS.md 23 is not checkable here."; exit 1; }; \
    [ ! -s /flux/sass_ptx.txt ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: FastEddy carries PTX. The -dlink finding may no longer hold;"; \
           echo "       re-check FASTEDDY_TRAPS.md 23 before trusting it."; exit 1; }

# THE JOB DIRECTORIES MUST BE CLEAN, or run_seed.sh refuses. A partial run on
# disk is refused rather than silently wiped (it may be hours of GPU someone
# wants), and a COMPLETE one is skipped and only re-scored. The .dockerignore
# excludes jobs*/*/output and jobs*/*/return for exactly that reason; this
# asserts that it worked, because a stale dump baked into the image would make
# every rented machine skip the LES and return a two-month-old restart.
# THE ASSERTION SEARCHES THE WHOLE TREE, NOT ONE GLOB LEVEL. Its first version globbed
# `/flux/jobs30/*/{output,return,FE_*}` and passed while 560 MB of FE_SMOKE dumps sat in
# `/flux/jobs/<seed>/smoke_aside/` -- excluded, it was assumed, by `jobs/*/FE_*`, which
# does not cross a second `/`. A guard that only looks where the pattern already looks
# cannot catch the pattern being wrong. `find` over everything is the check.
RUN set -eux; \
    # THE ONLY BULK ALLOWED UNDER jobs30 IS THE SEED LIBRARY ITSELF, AND IT IS COUNTED.
    # This assertion is the one that caught 560 MB of stray FE_SMOKE dumps, so relaxing it
    # for the 2.1 GB library must not blunt it: `*.nc` is allowed at exactly one path, and
    # anything else of any size at any depth is still fatal.
    STRAY=$(find /flux/jobs /flux/jobs30 \( -name 'FE_*' -o \( -name '*.nc' \
              -not -path '/flux/jobs30/*/return/seed_restart.nc' \) \) 2>/dev/null | head -20); \
    [ -z "$STRAY" ] || { echo "FATAL: baked job dirs carry run artifacts; fix .dockerignore"; \
                         echo "$STRAY"; exit 1; }; \
    BIG=$(du -sm /flux/jobs 2>/dev/null | awk '$1 > 20 {print}'); \
    [ -z "$BIG" ] || { echo "FATAL: /flux/jobs is over 20 MB, so something bulky is baked in:"; \
                       echo "$BIG"; exit 1; }; \
    test "$(ls -d /flux/jobs30/seed_* | wc -l)" = 30 || { echo "FATAL: expected 30 seed jobs"; exit 1; }; \
    # THE LIBRARY, ASSERTED AS AN ARTIFACT AND NOT AS A DIRECTORY LISTING. A corpus case
    # picks its seed per case, so a library that is 29 of 30 -- or 30 restarts one of which
    # is truncated -- produces plausible cases for 29 days and an unexplained failure on
    # the thirtieth, on a rented box, hours in. Every restart is the same size by
    # construction (one grid, one dump), so the size is checkable, not just the count.
    NR=$(ls /flux/jobs30/seed_*/return/seed_restart.nc 2>/dev/null | wc -l); \
    test "$NR" = 30 || { echo "FATAL: $NR seed restarts baked in, expected 30. A corpus"; \
                         echo "       case picks its seed per case; a partial library is"; \
                         echo "       an unexplained failure hours into a rental."; exit 1; }; \
    SZ=$(stat -c%s /flux/jobs30/seed_*/return/seed_restart.nc | sort -u); \
    test "$(echo "$SZ" | wc -l)" = 1 || { echo "FATAL: seed restarts differ in size, so at"; \
                                          echo "       least one is truncated:"; \
                                          echo "$SZ"; exit 1; }; \
    for f in manifest.json stationarity.json; do \
      N=$(ls /flux/jobs30/seed_*/return/$f 2>/dev/null | wc -l); \
      test "$N" = 30 || { echo "FATAL: $N of 30 seeds carry return/$f; bin/pick_seed.py"; \
                          echo "       reads both to rank a seed and would silently fall"; \
                          echo "       back to the index for the ones missing it."; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    # A CEILING ON THE WHOLE TREE, because the exclusions above are now a DENY LIST at
    # file level (a `!` re-include does not work under the classic builder) and a deny
    # list cannot see a file type nobody has added yet. 30 x 73.3 MB is 2.20 GB; anything
    # past 2.4 GB means something new is riding along.
    JM=$(du -sm /flux/jobs30 | cut -f1); \
    [ "$JM" -le 2400 ] || { echo "FATAL: /flux/jobs30 is ${JM} MB, over the 2400 MB ceiling."; \
                            echo "       The seed library is 30 x 73.3 MB = 2200 MB; something"; \
                            echo "       else is being baked in. .dockerignore excludes"; \
                            echo "       return/ FILE BY FILE, so a new artifact type is not"; \
                            echo "       covered until it is named."; \
                            du -sm /flux/jobs30/* | sort -rn | head -5; exit 1; }; \
    echo "seed library baked in: 30 restarts at $SZ bytes each, ${JM} MB total"; \
    # AND A CEILING ON THE WHOLE TREE, not just on jobs30. The per-directory ceiling above
    # cannot see a directory nobody has named: `vast-seeds/` -- the seed library exactly as
    # it came back off the rented box, a 2.1 GB duplicate of what jobs30 now holds -- was
    # baked into a build and passed every check, because every check named a path. The
    # payload is the 2.10 GB library plus ~60 MB of code, grids and vendored FFP.
    FM=$(du -sm /flux | cut -f1); \
    [ "$FM" -le 2400 ] || { echo "FATAL: /flux is ${FM} MB, over the 2400 MB ceiling."; \
                            echo "       Expected ~2160: the 2098 MB seed library plus code."; \
                            echo "       Something bulky is in the build context:"; \
                            du -sm /flux/* | sort -rn | head -8; exit 1; }; \
    python3 -c "import json;d=json.load(open('/flux/jobs30/index.json'));assert d['n_jobs']==30, d['n_jobs'];print('30 seed jobs, index.json consistent')"

# The provenance goes into the FILESYSTEM as well as into the labels, because
# `docker inspect` cannot be run from inside a container and the entrypoint has
# to be able to print what it is into a log someone will paste into a message.
RUN set -eux; \
    { echo "flux_commit        = ${GIT_COMMIT}"; \
      echo "fasteddy_commit    = ${GIT_COMMIT_FE}"; \
      echo "build_date         = ${BUILD_DATE}"; \
      echo "cuda               = $(nvcc --version | sed -n 's/.*release \([0-9.]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1)"; \
      echo "gcc                = $(gcc -dumpfullversion)"; \
      echo "cxx_dialect        = ${FE_STD}"; \
      echo "mpi                = $(ompi_info --version | head -1 | tr -s ' ')"; \
      echo "netcdf             = $(nc-config --version)"; \
      echo "fasteddy_sass      = $(tr '\n' ' ' < /flux/sass_elf.txt)"; \
      echo "fasteddy_ptx       = $(tr '\n' ' ' < /flux/sass_ptx.txt)  (empty by construction: nvcc -dlink drops PTX)"; \
      echo "liblpdm_sass       = $(tr '\n' ' ' < /flux/sass_elf_lpdm.txt)"; \
      echo "liblpdm_ptx        = $(tr '\n' ' ' < /flux/sass_ptx_lpdm.txt)  (whole-program, so it survives -- the contrast)"; \
      echo "numpy/scipy/netCDF4 = $(python3 -c 'import numpy,scipy,netCDF4;print(numpy.__version__, scipy.__version__, netCDF4.__version__)')"; \
      echo "hdf5_via_netcdf4   = $(python3 -c 'import netCDF4;print(netCDF4.__hdf5libversion__)')"; \
    } > /flux/IMAGE_PROVENANCE.txt; \
    cat /flux/IMAGE_PROVENANCE.txt

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Flux FastEddy seed generator" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="FastEddy v5.0.1 (kegonsa fork) + Kegonsa flux-footprint pipeline; CUDA 13.0; real SASS sm_75..sm_120" \
      org.opencontainers.image.revision="${GIT_COMMIT}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.created="${BUILD_DATE}" \
      flux.fasteddy.revision="${GIT_COMMIT_FE}" \
      flux.cuda="13.0.1" \
      flux.gencode="${FE_GENCODE}"

# FLUX_NATIVE=1 is what makes the project's own drivers run INSIDE a container
# instead of shelling out to `docker run` on a host. docker/run_case.sh and
# docker/pyrun.sh both branch on it; nothing else in the tree changes, and the
# host workflow is byte-for-byte what it was.
#
# THESE MUST BE REAL ENV, NOT ONLY ENTRYPOINT STATE, BECAUSE OF HOW VAST WORKS.
# Vast's SSH mode replaces the entrypoint, so anything the entrypoint used to
# export would never be set -- and an SSH login shell does not inherit `docker
# run -e` either. ENV lands in the image config, which Vast's injected
# entrypoint does propagate, and /etc/environment below covers the login-shell
# path as well. Both, because either alone has a hole.
# FLUX_COMMIT is the authority on the commit INSIDE the image. There is no .git here --
# the code is baked in and the tag IS the commit -- so bin/run_corpus_machine.py:git_commit
# reads this and stamps it into every machine manifest. Without it the manifest that
# outlives the rented box would not say which code produced the corpus.
ENV FLUX_ROOT=/flux \
    FLUX_NATIVE=1 \
    FLUX_OUT=/out \
    FLUX_COMMIT=${GIT_COMMIT} \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    PATH=/flux/bin:/flux/docker:${PATH}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO ENTRYPOINT. THIS IS A VAST.AI REQUIREMENT AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# Vast's own documentation: "With the SSH launch option your docker image
# entrypoint is not called as we must override it." So an ENTRYPOINT here is at
# best ignored and at worst something to fight. More importantly, the previous
# ENTRYPOINT + `CMD ["help"]` printed help and EXITED -- which on Vast is an
# instance that comes up and immediately dies.
#
# Instead: the subcommands become real executables on PATH, so
#
#     docker run --gpus all -v /out:/out <image> run_seeds --gpu-count 16
#
# still works exactly as before on a workstation (there is no entrypoint to
# prepend, and `run_seeds` resolves on PATH), while on Vast you SSH in and type
# `run_seeds --gpu-count 16` yourself. Nothing auto-runs, which is what was
# asked for: the on-start script field is deliberately left EMPTY.
#
# CMD is `sleep infinity` so that in Vast's "docker ENTRYPOINT" launch mode --
# and in a plain `docker run -d` -- the container stays up doing nothing,
# instead of exiting and taking the instance with it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /out /var/lock/flux /run/sshd; \
    for c in run_seeds run_corpus corpus_progress verify seed accept provenance flux-seeds; do \
      printf '#!/bin/sh\nexec /flux/docker/entrypoint.sh %s "$@"\n' \
        "$([ "$c" = flux-seeds ] && echo '' || echo "$c")" > "/usr/local/bin/$c"; \
      chmod +x "/usr/local/bin/$c"; \
    done; \
    # /etc/environment, because an SSH login shell inherits neither the image's
    # ENV nor `docker run -e`. Without this, `run_seeds` on a Vast box would run
    # with FLUX_NATIVE unset and shell out to a docker daemon that is not there.
    { echo "FLUX_ROOT=/flux"; echo "FLUX_NATIVE=1"; echo "FLUX_OUT=/out"; \
      echo "PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1"; \
      echo "PATH=/flux/bin:/flux/docker:/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/local/cuda/bin"; \
    } >> /etc/environment; \
    printf '%s\n' \
      '# Printed on every interactive login so a rented box says what it is.' \
      'if [ -n "$PS1" ] && [ -f /flux/IMAGE_PROVENANCE.txt ]; then' \
      '  echo; sed "s/^/  /" /flux/IMAGE_PROVENANCE.txt; echo;' \
      '  echo "  verify first (seconds, no GPU-hours):  verify"' \
      '  echo "  then the whole library:                run_seeds --gpu-count \$(nvidia-smi -L 2>/dev/null | wc -l)"' \
      '  echo "  results land in \$FLUX_OUT (/out). See /flux/DEPLOY.md."; echo;' \
      'fi' > /etc/profile.d/zz-flux.sh; \
    chmod 0644 /etc/profile.d/zz-flux.sh; \
    run_seeds --help > /dev/null 2>&1 || true; \
    test -x /usr/local/bin/run_seeds && test -x /usr/local/bin/verify \
      && test -x /usr/local/bin/run_corpus && test -x /usr/local/bin/corpus_progress

WORKDIR /flux
# ENTRYPOINT IS CLEARED EXPLICITLY, NOT MERELY LEFT UNSET.
# The nvidia/cuda base sets ENTRYPOINT ["/opt/nvidia/nvidia_entrypoint.sh"], which is
# INHERITED -- so "no ENTRYPOINT" is not what you get by writing none. That wrapper is
# benign (it prints the CUDA banner and execs "$@"), but the requirement here is that the
# image has nothing for Vast's injected entrypoint to interact with, and an inherited one
# is still one. `ENTRYPOINT []` is the only way to actually remove it.
ENTRYPOINT []
CMD ["sleep", "infinity"]
